# OpenCTEM {{ openctem_version }} — running this directory

Rendered by `scripts/render-standalone.yml` for `openctem_root={{ openctem_root }}`.
This server needs Docker with the compose plugin. It does not need Ansible,
Python, Galaxy collections or a registry login.

## 1. Put the directory in place

```sh
sudo mkdir -p {{ openctem_root }}
sudo cp -a ./ {{ openctem_root }}/
sudo chown -R root:root {{ openctem_root }}
cd {{ openctem_root }}
```

The path matters: `compose.yml` refers to `{{ openctem_root }}` by absolute
path. To use a different one, render again with `-e openctem_root=...`.

## 2. Images

`compose.yml` pins every image by digest, so the tag alone will not do.

Online: `docker compose pull`.

Offline: load the archives from `scripts/mirror-images.sh`. A digest survives
`docker load` only with Docker's containerd image store, which Docker 28 and
later use by default. Check it first, because without it the images arrive but
the pins resolve against nothing and the failure surfaces later, somewhere
unrelated:

```sh
docker info --format '{{ "{{json .DriverStatus}}" }}'   # expect io.containerd.snapshotter.v1
```

## 3. Secrets

```sh
sudo mkdir -p {{ openctem_secrets_dir }} && sudo chmod 0700 {{ openctem_secrets_dir }}
sudo scripts/openctem-secrets
```

Run it on this server, once. It generates the database and session secrets
here and prints file names, never values, so no password is created on the
machine that rendered this directory or carried it over. It is safe to re-run:
an existing secret is kept.
{% if openctem_ingress == 'cloudflared' %}

The tunnel token is yours to place, and this script never touches it:

```sh
sudo install -o 65532 -g 65532 -m 0400 /path/to/token {{ openctem_secrets_dir }}/cloudflared_tunnel_token
```
{% endif %}

## 4. Start

```sh
sudo docker compose config --quiet      # the file is valid and resolves
sudo docker compose up -d
sudo scripts/openctem-verify
```

{% if openctem_ingress == 'loopback' %}
The gateway publishes `{{ openctem_loopback_bind | default('127.0.0.1') }}:{{ openctem_loopback_port }}` in plain HTTP, for a TLS
reverse proxy you run yourself. That proxy owns TLS, must send
`X-Forwarded-For`, and is the only thing in front of the application.
{% else %}
Traffic arrives through the Cloudflare Tunnel connector; the host publishes no
port at all.
{% endif %}

## What you are taking on

The Ansible path does more than start containers, and none of it is here: host
hardening, a firewall, unattended security updates, time sync, file integrity
monitoring, backups with a tested restore, and monitoring. It also verifies the
cosign signature of every image before pulling it. Running the stack this way
is supported; the rest of that list becomes yours.

Upgrading means rendering again with the new `openctem_version` and repeating
steps 1, 2 and 4. Read the release notes first: a release that migrates the
schema is not reversible by starting the previous compose file.
